\section{What a Scanner Must Preserve}

Anthony Aaby's \textit{Compiler Construction using Flex and Bison} illustrates an extra policy found in scanners: among ordinary matching rules, prefer the longest lexeme, then the earliest rule on a tie. For keyword \texttt{if} and a general identifier rule, \texttt{ifx} is one identifier by length, while \texttt{if} can be a keyword by rule priority. A DFA that has retained only accepting versus rejecting states cannot implement that tie-breaker. Keep the winning rule at accepting states and retain the last accepting position until the current match cannot extend. This delay can be unbounded: with rules for $a$ and $ab^*c$, the scanner can read arbitrarily many $b$'s before learning that only the first rule matched. A finite recognition graph does not imply a bounded buffer or that arbitrary host actions are finite-state translations.

When changing a scanner, compare token kinds, spans, and error behavior, not merely whether every input is accepted by both versions. Test the keyword by itself, the keyword with an identifier suffix, and the keyword followed by punctuation. For the normalization machine, test rejection at end of input and check that every division of a sample into chunks produces the same final result as a single call. If the vocabulary is small, Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-132} gives an enumeration method that systematically supplies short examples rather than relying on a handful of plausible strings.

