\section{Minimizing States Without Changing When Output Appears}

An incremental translator may promise not just which output word is
produced, but when pieces of it become visible. For that stronger
contract, states cannot be merged merely because they accept the same
suffixes. They must also emit the same chunk on each next symbol,
and then continue from states that agree on future behavior.

\textbf{Contract.} Given a finite deterministic transducer over a
finite alphabet, partition its states so states in the same class
are interchangeable for every stream of symbols and end-of-input.
The observable trace includes one output chunk per consumed symbol,
including empty chunks, and a terminal record giving success or
failure and any final output. Use a total transition table; a
terminal reject sink can model failures reported only at end of
input. Immediate errors would need an additional observable action.

\begin{sudocode}[Refine by transition outputs and target classes]
\Let{P}{\Call{GroupByTerminalRecord}{Q}}
\Repeat
  \Let{old}{P}
  \For{each state $q$}
    \To{Retain the old class so this step only splits blocks.}
    \Let{K[q]}{\text{tuple starting with }old[q]}
    \For{each input symbol $a$ in fixed order}
      \To{Include the chunk, not just the destination.}
      \Let{c}{old[\delta(q,a)]}
      \State append $(\mathrm{emit}(q,a),c)$ to $K[q]$
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
  \Let{P}{\Call{GroupByEqualKey}{K}}
\Until{$P$ has the same blocks as $old$}
\State \Return $P$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Over the single symbol \(a\), states \(P\) and
\(Q\) both reject at end of input. Reading \(a\) from \(P\) emits
\(x\) and moves to \(R\), which accepts with no final output.
Reading \(a\) from \(Q\) emits \(\varepsilon\) and moves to \(S\),
which accepts with final output \(x\). Both \(R\) and \(S\) move
to a reject sink on further input. Each start translates only
\(a\) to \(x\), but the chunk pairs \((x,\varepsilon)\) and
\((\varepsilon,x)\) differ. The first refinement separates
\(P\) from \(Q\).

After \(k\) refinements, two states share a block exactly when
their observable traces agree for suffixes of length at most \(k\).
The base case checks end of input, and the key handles one more
symbol. Each strict round increases the block count, so the finite
procedure terminates.
Comparing blocks rather than their numeric IDs avoids spurious
changes from renumbering. If only concatenated output matters,
these classes can be finer than necessary because they preserve
the timing of emission.
